meta {
  name: Container recommendations - filter stale
  type: http
  seq: 243
}

get {
  url: {{baseURL}}/recommendations/openshift?filter[stale]=only&limit=20&offset=0
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

params:query {
  filter[stale]: only
  limit: 20
  offset: 0
}

headers {
  x-rh-identity: {{xRhIdentity}}
}

docs {
  filter[stale] — three states, not a boolean:
    false (default) — fresh rows only; stale rows are hidden from the list,
                      the counters and the savings totals.
    only            — the hidden rows, and nothing else. This is the escape
                      hatch for a cluster that disappeared from the default
                      view.
    true            — no staleness filter at all (fresh and stale together).
                      Despite the name, true does NOT mean "only stale".

  A row becomes stale on either of two clocks:
    ROS_CLUSTER_LIVENESS_HOURS (default 336 = 14 days) — the retention sweep
      marks both recommendation tables when every clusters row for that
      cluster reports older than the window. This is what catches a cluster
      that stopped reporting: it never runs its own cycle, so no per-cycle
      writer could mark it.
    ROS_STALENESS_THRESHOLD_HOURS (default 48) — the per-cycle writer marks a
      row whose composite key no longer appears in the cluster's digest.

  Marking is reversible: the next report clears stale, so a cluster that comes
  back returns to the default list on its own. Rows are deleted
  ROS_STALE_CLEANUP_DAYS (default 30) after the mark, so a decommissioned
  cluster leaves active figures at day 14 and is reclaimed at day 44.

  Legacy flat ?stale= is accepted for all three values.
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
  timeout: 0
}
